---
title: Interceptors
description: The four pipeline stages — pre, post, exception and final — how they compose, and the arities each one comes in.
---

Interceptors are how cross-cutting work attaches to a dispatch without the handler knowing
about it. They are discovered and registered exactly like handlers, and they run as part of the
same compiled pipeline.

## The four stages

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** runs before the handler, and may rewrite the message.
- **post** runs after a successful handler, and may rewrite the result.
- **exception** runs when the handler or an interceptor threw, and may supply a result instead.
- **final** always runs last, on success and on failure alike.

Within a stage, order is `[Weight]` descending, with direct registrations before covariant ones.
See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).

## Pre-interceptors

A pre-interceptor returns the message the next stage will see. Returning the input unchanged is
the pass-through case:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Two arities are available, trading breadth for type safety:

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, ErgosfareContext)` | it applies to every command |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<TCommand>(TCommand, ErgosfareContext)` | it applies to one command or hierarchy |

The typed form returns the typed message, so rewriting needs no cast:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

Returning a *derived* message is allowed and often the point — `TCommand` is the contract, and
anything assignable to it satisfies that:

```csharp
public sealed class Enrich : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult<PlaceOrder>(new ValidatedPlaceOrder(command));
}
```

{% aside type="note" %}
Earlier previews had a two-parameter form, `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>`. It
was removed: since the one-parameter form started returning the typed message, the second
argument bought nothing. Migration is a deletion — drop the second type argument and the
interceptor keeps returning what it returned.
{% /aside %}

## Post-interceptors

A post-interceptor sees a successful result and may replace it:

```csharp
public sealed class StampCreatedAt : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Three arities:

| Interface | Signature |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, ErgosfareContext)` |

For a void pipeline the result slot carries `Unit`, so a post-interceptor written against a
command with no result still has something well-defined to receive.

## Exception interceptors

An exception interceptor runs when something threw, and it can answer the dispatch with a result
instead of letting the exception escape:

```csharp
public sealed class TranslateNotFound
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptor<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct command, ProductDto? result, Exception exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (exception is NotFoundException)
            return ValueTask.FromResult(ProductDto.Empty);

        throw exception;
    }
}
```

Note the asymmetry, which is deliberate: **the result comes in nullable and goes out
non-nullable.** The handler may have thrown before producing anything, so there genuinely may
be no result yet — but a dispatch locked its result type at the call site, and no stage
downstream may hand the caller a `null` it never asked for.

An interceptor that cannot produce a result should not claim the failure. Rethrow, or do not
match; an unmatched stage lets the exception surface. Code that wants to model absence models
it in the value, which is what a result carrier is for.

Once any exception interceptor has run, the exception is considered handled and the pipeline
continues to the final stage. If none matched, it surfaces.

### Filtering by exception type

Implementing the `For` variant narrows an interceptor to one exception type, which reads better
than an `is` check and lets the compiled plan bake the test:

```csharp
public sealed class HandleTimeouts
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptorFor<PlaceOrder, Guid, TimeoutException>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, TimeoutException exception, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(Guid.Empty);
}
```

## Final interceptors

A final interceptor always runs — after success, after a handled failure, after an unhandled
one. It returns nothing, because by then the dispatch's answer is settled:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Both the result and the exception are nullable here, and for the same reason: it runs from a
`finally`, so either may be absent.

The one case where it does *not* run is a deliberate stop — see below.

## Stopping a pipeline

Any participant can end the dispatch early by throwing `ExecutionAbortedException`:

```csharp
public sealed class RejectUnauthorized : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.IsAuthenticated)
            throw new ExecutionAbortedException("not authenticated");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

This is not a failure and is not treated as one: the exception stage does not run, the final
stage does not run, and the signal travels to the caller. It says "this dispatch should not
continue", not "this dispatch went wrong".

## Which module's contracts to use

Each module has its own interceptor contracts — `ICommandPreInterceptor`,
`IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — and they carry that module's marker, which is
how discovery finds them.

For something genuinely universal, implement the core contracts in
`Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Handlers` against `IMessage`. That reaches every module's
messages, at the cost of a weaker static type.

## What this costs

Nothing per dispatch that you did not ask for. The stages are assembled at compile time, and
for a pipeline whose shape is fully settled, the generated plan is a straight line of calls
with the interceptors in place — no descriptor list, no ordering work, and for participants
provably constructible, no container resolution either.

A pipeline with no interceptors at all pays for none: it is the same straight line without the
calls.
